      "id": 1,
      "text": "Weesatche began as Middletown around 1850, positioned midway between Clinton and Goliad on the Old Goliad Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weesatche%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in November 1855, closed during the Civil War, and reopened in 1870.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/weesatche-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town renamed itself Weesatche after the sweet acacia tree, huisache, to avoid confusion with another Middletown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/weesatche-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Weesatche sits at 233 feet above sea level, a modest elevation typical of northern Goliad County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weesatche%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community is part of the Victoria, Texas Metropolitan Statistical Area, linking it to a larger regional economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
